[Music]  hi  my name is jaya ballou i'm the chief  information security officer  at avast and i'm also the quantum  ambassador  at kpn to introduce the european quantum  flagship in a few sentences would be to  explain  a massive project from the perspective  of the european union  we only have three flagships the human  brain  flagship the graphene flagship for new  materials for graphene  and now we have the quantum flagship and  the flagship  is investing a billion  euros in quantum research it's going on  for a period of  over 10 years there's more than 5 000  researchers and members of the academic  and commercial community that come  together  in order to realize quantum innovation  and development for european prosperity  and  jobs and our future so the main  objective of the european quantum  flagship is to have  innovation and research which propels  europe to be one of the top two  in the world in terms of having  innovation  for all of the quantum technologies from  computing  computation simulation and sensing and  meteorology  and this we believe will be the future  technology  that will propel us uh as humans  to embrace new advancements in science  so european quantum week runs from  november 2nd to the 6th it's running in  parallel with berlin science week  and basically it'll present all of the  work that we've been doing  on the quantum flagship in europe but  it'll also be  full of discussions both from an  academic level but also to community and  outreach so that it's also accessible  to not only scientists but more people  and talking about innovation and  development  and accessibility uh for educational  purposes  of all of the work around in quantum  that's what i find so inspiring  it's um it's this ability to do  something meaningful  that will help every european citizen  with underlying scientific breakthroughs  i think the there are a number of  unsolved questions and big challenges in  the field  and they're in you know the areas of  scientific challenges uh but they're  also engineering  uh challenges um for example uh for  developing a quantum computer we need to  fix issues with deco here and so that we  can have lots of qubits and  that it's still stable  and there's also questions around  society  how can we embrace all of the good  things that quantum computing will give  us  while understanding and mitigating any  potential downfalls  around for example cryptography which we  use every day all over the internet  so all of these challenges are  significant but i'm confident because of  the excellence  of research we have in europe will be  able to overcome them  i believe that the work that's being  done in europe around quantum  technologies  will change the world quantum  technologies are  fundamental for us to be able to better  understand  our universe they could help us  potentially solve  climate challenges that we're dealing  with now but also  covet 19 related challenges in terms of  drug development  quantum technologies can also be used to  improve  drug development and delivery so i  think that these things just those  things if the promise of quantum  computing comes true  it will radically change our planet

